  "why": "Every result before the snapshot settles a restriction of the planar question, so each is a case: the circle under total integrability, local perturbations of ellipses, polynomial integrability, centrally symmetric tables, and so on. The ones published in a journal of record count as digested. Most events after the snapshot change the question: cones in R^n, Kepler and two-centre billiards, complex caustics, a disc characterisation with no integrability hypothesis, and two unrelated namesake problems. Each of those is a variant. Surveys, numerics and formal normal-form results are none. The close call is openai-math-147. The statement leaves 'integrable' undefined, and caustic integrability near the boundary is the classical Birkhoff-Poritsky form, so the result could be read as whole. I answered case for three reasons. The manuscripts add C∞ smoothness and positive curvature. They cover only the near-boundary caustic and invariant-curve notions, leaving out first-integral and total-integrability readings. And the authors themselves call it the 'near-boundary Birkhoff conjecture', not the conjecture. Its AI role is generated: the README says the model produced the results. Its AI credit is read from that README's general statement about the repository, not from a quote specific to this family. The Lai-Lim-Ren models concern a different problem and are not credited here.",
